  <p>The production implication follows from the display time rather than from any dimension. A static open screen has 3 seconds and one frame. Whatever the brand needs a viewer to take away has to be in that frame, readable at a glance, before anyone has decided whether to tap.</p>

  <h2>TopView, and the sizes that are not TopView's</h2>

  <blockquote class="evidence">TopView runs a video of 10 to 60 seconds. The first 3 seconds play full screen with nothing on top, a conversion component fades in after that, and from the fourth second the ad sits as the first post in the feed. Four of five Douyin-specific sources give the 10 to 60 second window. The fifth gives 5 to 60, which is TikTok's published range.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: 5 Douyin-specific published sources, Chinese-language, pages dated 2019 to 2021 and 2025, collected 10 September 2026. Modal value, not a primary reading.</p>

  <p>That hand-off is the landing behavior, and it shapes the edit. The first 3 seconds are the only unobstructed stretch. After that a conversion component sits over the frame and the ad behaves like a feed post.</p>

