  <p>The last row is the important one, and here is where the gap usually gets filled from.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The seven pixel sizes English guides list as TopView sizes, running from 480 by 800 to 1080 by 2340, are the video file set for the 5-second open screen in a Douyin rate card from the fourth quarter of 2020. No Chinese-language source attaches them to TopView. No Douyin-specific source publishes a TopView file size, resolution or bitrate at all.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the 2020 rate card read in full and compared with English search extracts on 10 September 2026, against 16 Chinese-language sources. An observation about provenance, not a primary reading.</p>

  <p>And the Chinese-language search has its own substitution.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Search in Chinese for Douyin's TopView specs and the top result is TikTok's own TopView help page. Its numbers are TikTok's: a 540 by 960 pixel floor, a 500MB cap, a 100-character title. It never mentions Douyin, and one of its rules, no pure white in the first 3 seconds, exists because of where TikTok puts its logo.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: one Chinese-language query run 10 September 2026, the result read in full, page updated June 2026. An observation about the search results, not a Douyin spec.</p>

  <p>So a TopView brief built from search results arrives with either a 2020 open screen file list or a TikTok spec sheet attached. Neither is a Douyin TopView spec, and the page publishes none rather than borrow one.</p>

  <h2>What size is a Douyin in-feed ad?</h2>

  <p>A vertical 9:16 video, with the recent sources agreeing on the floor and the older ones not.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">For in-feed video, the sources that agree are the recent ones. Pages dated 2023 and 2024 put the vertical floor at 720 by 1280 in 9:16. Pages from 2018 to 2021 say 540 by 960. Duration moved the same way: 5 to 30 seconds in 2018, 5 to 60 on four pages dated 2019 to 2023, and 4 to 300 seconds on the only page dated 2024.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: 7 published sources, Chinese-language, collected 10 September 2026, grouped by publication date before counting. Modal values, not primary readings.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">No file size cap for in-feed video settles. It appears as 500MB on two sources, 1,000MB on the only 2024 page, and 100MB on one more. A file under 100MB clears every figure in circulation.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: 4 published sources, Chinese-language, collected 10 September 2026, the full spread reported. Modal values, not primary readings; the last sentence is reasoning from the smallest published figure.</p>

